{"name":"Thresher Shark","hex":"#93cce7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"thresher shark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the species of sharks in the genus Alopias, three of which are extant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/thresher_shark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thresher shark","blocks":[{"text":"Thresher sharks are large mackerel sharks of the family Alopiidae found in all temperate and tropical oceans of the world; the family contains three extant species, all within the genus Alopias."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Pelagic_thresher_shark_%28Alopias_pelagicus%29.jpg/330px-Pelagic_thresher_shark_%28Alopias_pelagicus%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thresher_shark"},{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/330px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.o